Summary: A graph \(G\) is called an interval if there exist \(a, b\in V(G)\) such that \(G\) is the union of shortest paths connecting \(a\) and \(b\). In this paper, we show that
\begin{itemize}
\item[1.] If \(G\) is an interval between \(a\) and \(b\), then there exists a path \(H\) with diameter \(d(H) = d(G)\) such that there is a homomorphism \(f: G\to H\) and the distance \(\rho(a, b) + 1 \leq |H| \leq |G| - 1\).
\item[2.] Every interval is a connected bipartite graph.
\item[3.] If \(G\) is an interval between \(a\) and \(b\) that is not a path, then \(G\) has a path \(P\) with internal vertices (if any) all of degree 2 such that deletion of the internal vertices of \(P\) from \(G\) gives rise to an interval (if \(P = uv\) then \(G - uv\) is an interval).
\end{itemize}

Let \(G\) be a finite group, \(S\) a symmetric generating set of \(G\) and let \(\mathrm{Cay}(G,S)\) be the Cayley graph over \(G\) with connection set \(S\). \textit{L. Babai} and \textit{Á. Serres} [Eur. J. Comb. 13, No. 4, 231--243 (1992; Zbl 0783.20001)] conjectured that \(\mathrm{diam} \big (\mathrm{Cay}(G,S) \big)=(\log|G|)^{O(1)}\), uniformly over all nonabelian finite simple groups \(G\) and symmetric generating sets \(S\).

By the classification of finite simple groups, Babai's conjecture splits into essentially three broad cases:
\begin{itemize}
\item[1.] Groups of Lie type of bounded rank over \(F_{q}\) with \(q \rightarrow \infty\);
\item[2.] Classical groups of unbounded rank over \(F_{q}\) with \(q\) arbitrary;
\item[3.] Alternating groups \(A_{n}\) with \(n \rightarrow \infty\).
\end{itemize}
For groups of Lie type and bounded rank, Babai's conjecture is now completely resolved [\textit{H. A. Helfgott}, Ann. Math. (2) 167, No. 2, 601--623 (2008; Zbl 1213.20045); \textit{E. Breuillard}, \textit{B. Green} and \textit{T. Tao}, Geom. Funct. Anal. 21, No. 4, 774--819 (2011; Zbl 1229.20045); \textit{L. Pyber} and \textit{E. Szabó}, J. Am. Math. Soc. 29, No. 1, 95-146 (2016; Zbl 1371.20010)]).

Babai's conjecture is open for alternating groups (the main result in this case is a quasipolynomial bound of \textit{H. A. Helfgott} and \textit{Á. Seress} [Ann. Math. (2) 179, No. 2, 611--658 (2014; Zbl 1295.20027)]).

The case of classical groups of unbounded rank is still wide open. The best bound currently known is \(\mathrm{diam} \big (\mathrm{Cay}(G, S) \big ) \leq q^{O(n(\log n)^{2})}\) due to \textit{Z. Halasi} et al. [Bull. Lond. Math. Soc. 51, No. 4, 645--657 (2019; Zbl 1454.20092)]. By contrast, Babai's conjecture in this case asserts that \(\mathrm{diam} \big (\mathrm{Cay}(G, S) \big ) \leq (n \log q)^{O(1)}\).

The paper under review is a strong contribution toward a proof of the conjecture of Babai under the additional hypothesis that the connection set contains random elements. The authors prove that Babai's conjecture holds in the following cases:
\begin{itemize}
\item[1.] \(\mathrm{SL}_{n}(p)\), \(p\) prime, \( \log p < cn/ \log^{2} n\), and there are at least 3 random generators in the connection set;
\item[2.] \(\mathrm{SCl}_{n}(q)\) and there are at least \(q^{C}\) random generators in the connection set, where \(C\) is an absolute constant and \[ \mathrm{SCl}_{n}(q) \in \big \{\mathrm{SL}_{n}(q), \mathrm{Sp}_{n}(q),  \Omega_{n}(q), \Omega^{+}_{n}(q),  \Omega^{-}_{n}(q),  \mathrm{SU}_{n}(q) \big \}. \]
\end{itemize}
The authors remark that their proof does not depend on the classification of finite simple groups in any way.
